VPC (Virtual Private Cloud):
is a dedicated network on the cloud that you can use to place your servers, firewalls and internet gateway in case you want to add some instances to be available on the network, and you can segment your vpc so that you can create for example a dmz to isolate the internal network from the public network.

Internet gateway:
connects your instances that are placed in your vpc to the internet. and you can also network access control through it.

NOTE: we can divide our VPC into several network segements, each one have an ip range and each one can either be a public or a private subnet, the public subnet is connected to a internet gateway and all the instances on that subnet has a public ip address.

NOTE: in order for a machine that is setting on the private subnet to access the internet we have to place a NAT Gateway which will assign an elastic IP to this machine on the public subnet so that it can access the internet, the NAT gateway only allow outbound traffic and not traffic that connects from outbound.

NACL (Network Access contorl list):
allows you to put a list of rules that applies to ingress and egress traffic on the vpc.

NOTE: very important thing to note here is that rules are numbered and they are applied from lowest to highest, in other words the rule that matches the traffic will applied and any other rule afterwards will not.

NOTE: rules are stateless: Responses to ingress traffic are subject to egress rules.

in Amazon the virtual machine is called EC2 instance. you can control the ingress and egress traffic of the EC2 instance. it's stateful.

NOTE: All rules are evaluated, and the most permissive rule is used to make a decision.

NOTE: secure cloud archeticture recommends to have 2 seperate vpcs one for production and another one for management that contains the rdp gateways to admin instances.


